29:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-w-0 flex-1 flex-col gap-16","children":[["$","section","01-beyond-one-cpu",{"id":"01-beyond-one-cpu","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"01"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Beyond one CPU: the landscape"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"When we say a system has \"more than one CPU\", we could mean three quite different things, and conflating them is the first mistake to avoid. The useful way to organise them is by how tightly the processors are coupled, how much they share and how tied together their fates are. As we loosen that coupling we gain the ability to scale to enormous size, and we pay with coordination problems that get progressively nastier."}],["$","dl","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Multiprocessor (shared memory)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Several CPU cores inside one machine, all reading and writing one physical memory, managed by a single OS. Your phone and laptop are here. Tightest coupling."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Multicomputer (cluster)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Many complete machines, each with its own CPU and its own private memory, tightly wired by a fast interconnect and cooperating by sending messages. No shared memory. HPC supercomputers live here."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Distributed system"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Independent computers, often in different racks, rooms, or continents, cooperating over a general network. No shared memory, no shared clock, and no shared fate, each can fail on its own. Loosest coupling."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The single most important axis hiding in that table is shared fate. In a multiprocessor, if the machine dies, everything dies together, you never have to reason about \"half the cores are up\". In a distributed system the whole point is that one node can vanish while the rest carry on, which sounds like a feature (and it is) but turns out to be the source of almost every hard problem in the field. Hold that idea; the last three sections are essentially its consequences."}],["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The through-line",": "]}],"One box, one memory, one OS → many boxes, private memories, messages → many independent machines that fail one at a time. Everything in this chapter is a point on that line."]}]]}]]}],"$L2b"]}]]}],"$L2c","$L2d","$L2e","$L2f","$L30","$L31","$L32"]}]
2a:["$","$L33",null,{"prev":{"slug":"containers-namespaces-cgroups","title":"Containers, Namespaces & cgroups","kind":"$undefined","number":32},"next":{"slug":"case-study-linux-android","title":"Case Study: Linux & Android","kind":"$undefined","number":34}}]
34:I[76156,["2619","static/chunks/2619-04bc32f026a0d946.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs","6238","static/chunks/app/operating-system/%5Bchapter%5D/page-6f5feff9906adfcd.js?dpl=dpl_7RkrhxkHMBxRwELcvSCn238CEijs"],"NoteImage"]
2b:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F01-beyond-one-cpu.jpg?v=de633d2908b35d04a7088271a0bed3f5","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a left-to-right spectrum in three panels. Left panel \"multiprocessor\", one box holding four CPU cores all wired to a single shared RAM under one OS label. Middle panel \"multicomputer / cluster\", four boxes each with its own CPU and its own RAM, joined by a fast interconnect, arrows labelled \"messages\". Right panel \"distributed system\", four separate machines drawn far apart, connected by a cloud-shaped network, each with a small lightning bolt labelled \"can fail alone\". A banner underneath reads \"coupling loosens left to right; scale and difficulty both grow\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
2c:["$","section","02-smp",{"id":"02-smp","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"02"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Multiprocessors: SMP and one OS across many cores"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The dominant shape of a modern multiprocessor is SMP, symmetric multiprocessing. \"Symmetric\" means every core is equal: there is no master core, any core can run user code or kernel code, and they all reach the same physical memory through a shared interconnect. Critically, there is one operating system, one kernel image, cooperatively driving all the cores at once. When you buy an 8-core laptop, you are buying an SMP machine, and Linux runs a single kernel across all eight."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is wonderful for programmers because the mental model barely changes: threads still share one address space, memory still looks like one flat array, and a thread can be picked up by any idle core. But running one kernel on many cores at once resurrects, at the kernel’s own level, the exact problem we studied in the concurrency chapters. Two cores can try to modify the same kernel data structure, the run queue of ready threads, the open-file table, a memory allocator’s free list, at literally the same instant."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"So the scheduler you met earlier becomes a multi-core scheduler. Rather than one global run queue that every core fights over (a lock everyone contends for, which does not scale), modern kernels keep a per-core run queue and periodically balance load between them, pulling a waiting thread onto a core that has gone idle. And every shared kernel structure that two cores might touch has to be protected by exactly the locking discipline from the synchronization chapter, except now the \"threads\" contending are whole CPUs, and a lock held too long stalls real hardware."}],["$","ul","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"One kernel drives all cores; any core can execute kernel code, so kernel data structures are shared and must be synchronized."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Per-core run queues with periodic load balancing beat a single global run queue, because a global queue becomes a contended lock that caps scalability."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Coarse locks are simple but serialize the kernel; fine-grained locks scale better but risk deadlock and are far harder to get right, the same trade-off you met for application code."}]]}],"$L35"]}],"$L36"]}],"$L37"]}]]}]
2d:["$","section","03-cache-coherence",{"id":"03-cache-coherence","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"03"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Cache coherence & memory consistency"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Here is the catch buried inside that friendly SMP picture. Each core has its own private caches (L1, L2) for speed, and those caches hold copies of memory. So the same memory address can sit in two cores’ caches at once. If core 0 writes a new value into its cache and core 1 keeps reading its own stale copy, the two cores now disagree about what memory says, and the tidy illusion of one shared memory shatters. This is the cache coherence problem."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Hardware solves it with a coherence protocol that the cores run among themselves over the interconnect, so that software mostly does not have to think about it. The classic one is MESI, named for the four states each cached line can be in. You do not need to implement it, but the intuition pays off when you reason about performance."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Modified"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"This core has the only copy and has changed it; memory is now stale. The core must write back before anyone else can read."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Exclusive"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"This core has the only copy and it matches memory. It can write freely without telling anyone (no other cache to invalidate)."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Shared"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Several cores hold identical read-only copies. Fine for reading; a write here forces the next state change."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Invalid"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"This copy is stale and must not be used. A core reaching this line must re-fetch a fresh copy."}]]}]]}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The mechanism is simple to state: when a core wants to write a line that other caches hold, it broadcasts an invalidate, flipping every other copy to Invalid, so its write becomes the single source of truth. That keeps everyone honest, but it also means a write shared across cores is not free; it triggers cross-core traffic. This is why heavily shared, frequently written variables (a global counter behind every request, say) can quietly become a scaling bottleneck: the cache line ping-pongs between cores."}],"$L38","$L39","$L3a"]}],"$L3b"]}]]}]
2e:["$","section","04-numa",{"id":"04-numa","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"04"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"NUMA: when not all memory is equally close"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The neat SMP picture assumes every core reaches memory at the same speed. That holds for small machines, but once you scale to big servers with many sockets and lots of RAM, a single shared bus becomes a bottleneck, every core funnelling through one path cannot keep up. So large machines are built as several NUMA nodes: each node bundles a group of cores with a slice of memory that is physically attached to them, and the nodes are joined by an interconnect. NUMA stands for Non-Uniform Memory Access, and the non-uniformity is the whole point."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"It is still one shared address space, any core can read any address, and coherence still holds across the whole machine. But the cost is no longer flat. Reaching memory on your own node (local access) is fast; reaching memory attached to another node (remote access) means a trip across the interconnect and is meaningfully slower, often by a large factor. The address is the same to your program; the latency is not."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This turns placement into a performance decision the OS must make, and it connects two chapters at once. The memory allocator wants to satisfy a thread’s allocation from its local node (a common policy is \"first touch\": the page is placed on the node of whichever core first writes it). The scheduler, meanwhile, wants to keep a thread running on the node where its memory already lives, and to avoid migrating a thread to a distant node where every memory access would suddenly go remote. When the allocator and scheduler cooperate, a NUMA machine flies; when a thread and its data drift onto different nodes, the same code silently slows down."}],["$","ul","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Local memory access is fast; remote access crosses the interconnect and is slower, same instruction, different cost depending on where the page physically lives."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"NUMA-aware allocation (\"first touch\") places a page on the node of the core that first uses it, so hot data starts out local."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"NUMA-aware scheduling keeps a thread on the node holding its memory and resists migrating it to a distant node."}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":["$L3c","$L3d"]}]]}],"$L3e"]}],"$L3f"]}]]}]
2f:["$","section","05-multicomputers-mpi",{"id":"05-multicomputers-mpi","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"05"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Multicomputers & message passing"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Take the multiprocessor and cut the wire to shared memory. Now you have many complete computers, each with its own CPU and its own private RAM, bolted together by a very fast interconnect. This is a multicomputer, and its defining feature is what it lacks: there is no memory that two nodes can both touch. A node cannot learn what another node computed by reading a shared variable, because no such variable exists. The only way to communicate is to send a message."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That flips the programming model. In shared-memory code, communication is implicit and free-looking, you write to memory, another thread reads it (with all the coherence and locking caveats above). In message-passing code, communication is explicit: one node calls something like send() with a chunk of data, another calls recv() to receive it, and the data is physically copied across the interconnect. There is nothing to lock, because there is nothing shared; but there is nothing free either, because every exchange is an explicit transfer you must design."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is the world of high-performance computing, the supercomputers that run weather models, physics simulations, and, increasingly, large-scale training jobs. The dominant standard for writing such programs is MPI (the Message Passing Interface): a library that gives you send/receive between thousands of nodes plus collective operations (broadcast a value to everyone, sum a value across everyone, and so on). You do not need MPI’s API memorised; you need the shift in mindset it represents."}],["$","dl","3",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Shared memory (SMP)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Communicate by reading and writing the same memory. Implicit, fast, but needs locks and coherence; limited to one machine."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Message passing (multicomputer)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Communicate by explicit send/recv of data copied over the interconnect. No shared state to lock, but every exchange is deliberate; scales to thousands of nodes."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"MPI"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The Message Passing Interface, the standard library for writing message-passing programs across the nodes of an HPC cluster."}]]}],"$L40"]}],"$L41"]}],"$L42"]}]]}]
30:["$","section","06-distributed-shift",{"id":"06-distributed-shift","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"06"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Distributed systems: the shift and its defining difficulty"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"A distributed system loosens the coupling one final notch. Now the cooperating computers are fully independent, separate machines, often in different racks or datacenters, joined by an ordinary network rather than a dedicated interconnect. This is the shape of essentially every real backend: web servers, databases, caches, queues, each a separate machine (or many), all talking over the network. And here three assumptions you have relied on for the entire course quietly disappear."}],["$","ul","1",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"No shared memory: a machine knows only what it has been told in a message. There is no global variable, no shared data structure, every fact about another node is second-hand and possibly already out of date."}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"No shared clock: each machine has its own clock, and they drift. \"Which event happened first?\" has no reliable answer from timestamps alone, which breaks a surprising amount of naïve reasoning."}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"No shared fate, the defining one: any single machine can fail, or the network between two machines can fail, while everything else keeps running. This is partial failure, and it is the heart of what makes distributed systems hard."}]]}]]}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Sit with partial failure, because it is genuinely new. On one machine, a component either works or the whole machine crashes with it, there is no middle. Across a network there is a permanent, unavoidable middle: you send a request to another node and no reply comes back. Now answer one question, what happened? Maybe the node crashed before receiving it. Maybe it received and did the work but the reply was lost. Maybe it is merely slow and the reply is still coming. Maybe the network between you is cut but the node is perfectly healthy and serving others. From where you stand these are indistinguishable, yet they demand different responses, and this ambiguity is why distributed algorithms are so careful and so counter-intuitive."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Because these traps are so easy to walk into, the field has a famous list: the eight fallacies of distributed computing, false assumptions that engineers new to distribution make by reflex. A few worth burning in: the network is reliable (it is not, packets drop, links fail); latency is zero (a remote call is orders of magnitude slower than a local one, so chatty designs crawl); bandwidth is infinite (it is not); the network is secure (assume it is hostile); and topology never changes (machines come and go constantly). Every one of these has produced real outages when assumed away."}],"$L43"]}],"$L44"]}]]}]
31:["$","section","07-consistency-consensus-cap",{"id":"07-consistency-consensus-cap","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"07"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Hard problems: ordering, consensus & CAP"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Once machines are independent, three deep problems show up again and again. You will not derive their solutions here, each is a field of its own, but you should know the names, the shape of each problem, and why it is genuinely hard, so the tools you use later stop looking like magic."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"First, ordering. With no shared clock, how do we agree on the order events happened across machines? Wall-clock timestamps lie because clocks drift. The classic answer is logical clocks: Lamport’s insight that we do not need real time, only a consistent notion of \"this event happened before that one\". Each node keeps a counter it bumps on every event and piggybacks on every message; the rule that a send always precedes its receive lets everyone agree on a partial order of events without any shared clock. It is a small idea with enormous reach, it underpins how distributed databases and version-control-like systems reason about causality."}],["$","p","2",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Second, consensus: how do a group of machines agree on a single value, who is the leader, what is the next entry in the log, did this transaction commit, when messages can be lost or delayed and nodes can crash mid-vote? This is mutual exclusion’s harder cousin, and it is provably subtle. The workhorse algorithms are Paxos (correct, famously hard to understand) and Raft (designed to be understandable, now the default in new systems). The common trick is a majority quorum: get more than half the nodes to agree and you can tolerate a minority failing without ever letting two different values both \"win\". Raft and Paxos are what sit underneath systems like etcd, ZooKeeper, and the replication in modern distributed databases."}],["$","p","3",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Third, the CAP theorem, the intuition every backend engineer should carry. Picture three properties: Consistency (every read sees the latest write, as if there were one copy), Availability (every request gets a non-error response), and Partition-tolerance (the system keeps working even when the network splits nodes into groups that cannot talk). CAP says that when a network partition happens, and on a real network it eventually will, you cannot have both C and A. You must choose: either refuse some requests to avoid returning stale or conflicting data (choose consistency, sacrifice availability), or keep answering from each side of the split and reconcile later (choose availability, sacrifice consistency). Partition-tolerance is not really optional on a real network, so CAP is best read as: \"when, not if, the network partitions, do you drop consistency or availability?\"."}],"$L45","$L46"]}],"$L47"]}]]}]
32:["$","section","08-why-engineers-care",{"id":"08-why-engineers-care","className":"scroll-mt-8","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-5 flex items-baseline gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold text-accent/60","children":"08"}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-hand text-3xl font-bold leading-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl","children":"Why engineers care: the same themes, one box to a datacenter"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"grid gap-7 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,340px)] lg:items-start","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-col gap-4","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Step back and the payoff of putting this chapter last becomes clear: the coordination themes recur at every scale, and knowing the OS-level version gives you the datacenter-level version almost for free. The mutex that guards a shared variable between two threads is the same idea as a distributed lock that stops two service instances from processing the same job, except now \"held too long\" means a network timeout and \"the holder crashed\" is a real, routine event you must plan for. Cache coherence across cores is the small-scale rehearsal for replica consistency across nodes. The OS scheduling threads onto cores is mirrored by an orchestrator like Kubernetes scheduling containers onto machines."}],["$","p","1",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"This is why the abstractions you build on daily are shaped the way they are. Microservices are just message-passing between independent, separately-failing nodes, with all of Section 6’s partial-failure problems baked in, which is exactly why they need retries, timeouts, and idempotent handlers. A distributed database or a coordination service like etcd earns its keep by running the consensus algorithms of Section 7 so you do not have to. A distributed lock or leader election is mutual exclusion stretched across machines that can crash. None of it is new physics; it is the OS concurrency toolkit re-derived where the participants are whole computers."}],["$","dl","2",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Distributed lock"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"A mutex whose scope is many machines (built on etcd, ZooKeeper, or Redis). Must handle a holder that crashes or a network that partitions, the on-one-box mutex never had to."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Microservices"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Independent services cooperating by messages over the network. Inherit every partial-failure problem, which is why resilient design (retries, timeouts, idempotency, circuit breakers) is mandatory, not optional."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Replication & consensus"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Keeping copies of state across nodes consistent, using quorum-based algorithms (Raft/Paxos). The scaled-up cousin of cache coherence."}]]}]]}],"$L48","$L49","$L4a"]}],"$L4b"]}]]}]
35:["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Cross-references: scheduling (the run queue and how a thread is chosen) and locks/mutexes (protecting the shared structures) are the two prior chapters you are now applying at kernel scale."}]]}]
36:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The lesson repeats",": "]}],"SMP does not introduce a new kind of hard problem, it takes the concurrency problems you already know and makes the OS itself the concurrent program. Everything about races, critical sections, and lock contention applies to the kernel running on many cores."]}]]}]
37:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F02-smp.jpg?v=8c064ac20d6edba6c5d2e371ca8dbbe8","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: an SMP layout, four CPU cores in a row, each with its own small L1/L2 cache box, all connected downward to a single shared bus, which connects to one big shared RAM block. Above the cores, one rounded box labelled \"single OS kernel, one copy\". To the side, a \"run queue\" drawn as a list of ready threads with arrows pulling threads onto idle cores, and a red padlock icon on a shared kernel data structure labelled \"must be locked\". A caption: \"symmetric = every core is equal, any core can run kernel code\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
38:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"The subtlest trap is false sharing. Caches move data in fixed-size lines (commonly 64 bytes), not individual variables. If two threads on two cores update two different variables that happen to sit in the same cache line, the hardware cannot tell they are logically unrelated, every write invalidates the other core’s copy of the whole line, and the line bounces back and forth as if the threads were fighting over one variable. The code looks perfectly parallel and runs shockingly slowly. The fix is to pad or align hot per-thread data so unrelated fields land on separate cache lines."}]
39:["$","p","5",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"Coherence answers \"do all cores eventually agree on the value at an address?\". A second, distinct question, memory consistency, asks \"in what order does one core see another core’s writes become visible?\". This is exactly the memory-model material from the previous chapter: because CPUs and compilers reorder operations for speed, a plain write by one thread may become visible to another out of program order. That is why concurrent code needs memory barriers and atomics, and why the language memory model exists. Coherence is about which value; consistency is about which order."}]
3a:["$","div","6",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Engineer’s takeaway",": "]}],"When a lock-free or \"obviously parallel\" data structure scales badly, suspect the cache line before the algorithm. Count how often a single line is written from multiple cores, watch for false sharing on adjacent fields, and remember: correctness across cores still requires the atomics and barriers from the memory-model chapter, coherent caches do not give you ordering for free."]}]]}]
3b:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F03-cache-coherence.jpg?v=3652ed6047d6b96b195389b78c0c72b8","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: two CPU cores side by side, each with a private cache box. Both caches hold a copy of the same variable X = 5, drawn as duplicated sticky notes with a two-way arrow between them labelled \"same address, two copies\". Core 0 writes X = 6; a bold arrow labelled \"invalidate!\" shoots to Core 1’s copy, which gets crossed out. Below, a small MESI state wheel with four labels Modified / Exclusive / Shared / Invalid. To the right, a \"false sharing\" panel: one 64-byte cache line drawn as a long box split into two halves labelled var A and var B, two cores each hammering one half, and a ping-pong arrow bouncing the whole line back and forth with a sad face."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
3c:["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-[2px] select-none text-accent","children":"▸"}]
3d:["$","span",null,{"children":"On Linux, tools like numactl and the kernel’s automatic NUMA balancing exist precisely to keep threads and their pages on the same node."}]
3e:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why big servers care",": "]}],"On a large database or in-memory cache server, NUMA effects can swing throughput dramatically. If you run high-performance software on a multi-socket box and ignore NUMA, you can leave a big fraction of the machine’s performance on the table without a single line of your code looking wrong."]}]]}]
3f:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F04-numa.jpg?v=be22da760cce82eb2bf079e0d95ef01e","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: two NUMA nodes drawn as two rounded regions. Node 0 contains several cores plus a local RAM block, joined by a short thick arrow labelled \"local, fast ~100ns\". Node 1 likewise has cores and its own RAM. A long thin arrow crosses between the two nodes labelled \"remote, slower, crosses the interconnect\". A thread token sits on a Node 0 core with its memory pages drawn as small squares; a good case shows the pages on Node 0 (green check) and a bad case shows the same thread’s pages stranded on Node 1 (red cross, long arrow). Caption: \"same address space, different distances\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
40:["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Interconnect"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"The fast, tightly-coupled network joining the nodes (e.g. InfiniBand). Faster and more specialised than a general datacenter network."}]]}]
41:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Bridge to what is next",": "]}],"Message passing between tightly-coupled, reliable nodes is the stepping stone to distributed systems. Keep the send/recv model, then take away the reliable interconnect and the shared fate. What is left is the far messier world of independent machines, which the rest of this chapter is about."]}]]}]
42:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F05-multicomputers-mpi.jpg?v=771c6914807a7f29f1a804d036c71c40","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a contrast panel. Left side \"SMP, shared memory\" shows two cores both pointing arrows at one shared RAM box holding a variable, labelled \"communicate by reading/writing the same memory\". Right side \"multicomputer, message passing\" shows two separate boxes, each with its own CPU and its own private RAM, joined by a fast interconnect; an envelope icon travels along the link with send() leaving one box and recv() catching it at the other. Below, a rack of many such nodes labelled \"HPC cluster\", and a small note \"MPI = the standard for writing these programs\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
43:["$","div","4",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["The one thing to remember",": "]}],"Partial failure is the essence of distributed computing. A missing reply does not tell you whether the work was done. Designing systems that behave correctly despite that ambiguity, retries that are safe to repeat, timeouts, idempotency, is most of the job."]}]]}]
44:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F06-distributed-shift.jpg?v=4b86c3264ee3845daabd025d2eb9e80e","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: several independent machines drawn as separate computers, spread out and joined by a lossy cloud-shaped network with a few dashed, broken links. Each machine has its own little wall clock showing a slightly different time (no shared clock) and its own RAM (no shared memory). One machine is greyed out with an X and a question mark bubble reading \"crashed? or just slow? or network cut?\" pointing from a healthy neighbour that cannot tell. A side list titled \"fallacies\" with three ticked-off myths: \"the network is reliable\", \"latency is zero\", \"the network is secure\", each struck through."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
45:["$","dl","4",{"className":"flex flex-col gap-2.5","children":[["$","div","0",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Logical clocks (Lamport)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Counters that establish a consistent \"happened-before\" order across machines without any shared or synchronized real-time clock."}]]}],["$","div","1",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Consensus (Raft / Paxos)"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Algorithms letting a group agree on one value despite lost messages and crashed nodes, typically via a majority quorum. Raft favours understandability; Paxos came first."}]]}],["$","div","2",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"CAP theorem"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Under a network partition you can guarantee Consistency or Availability but not both; since partitions are inevitable, you are really choosing which to give up when one occurs."}]]}],["$","div","3",{"className":"rounded-lg border border-ink/12 bg-surface/60 px-3.5 py-2.5","children":[["$","dt",null,{"className":"font-hand text-xl font-bold leading-tight text-accent2","children":"Quorum"}],["$","dd",null,{"className":"mt-0.5 text-[0.97rem] leading-[1.55] text-ink/80","children":"Requiring agreement from a majority (more than half) so the system tolerates a minority of failures while never letting two conflicting decisions both stand."}]]}]]}]
46:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Why distributed state is hard",": "]}],"Keeping one logical value consistent across many machines that can each crash and cannot trust their clocks is the core difficulty. Ordering, consensus, and CAP are three faces of it. This is why \"just replicate the database\" is never as simple as it sounds, and why the algorithms that do it correctly are celebrated."]}]]}]
47:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F07-consistency-consensus-cap.jpg?v=b76bf270eb6c7098a932c8805e512c5a","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: three linked panels. Left \"ordering\", two nodes exchanging messages with Lamport logical-clock counters incrementing on each send/receive, arrows showing \"happened-before\". Middle \"consensus\", five node circles voting, a majority of three shaded and joined to agree on a single value, labelled \"Raft / Paxos: a majority agrees\". Right \"CAP\", a large triangle with vertices labelled Consistency, Availability, Partition-tolerance, and a note \"when the network partitions, keep C or A, not both\" with the P vertex circled as unavoidable."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
48:["$","div","3",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["Where the wire details live",": "]}],"This chapter kept the network deliberately light, we talked about messages, partitions, and latency, not packets. How data actually crosses the wire (TCP reliability, DNS, TLS, routing) is a whole subject of its own, covered in depth in the companion Computer Networking course. When you need the protocol-level detail beneath \"send a message to another node\", that is where to go."]}]]}]
49:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-[1.02rem] leading-[1.75] text-ink/90","children":"That closes Part F. You have taken the single-machine OS, processes, scheduling, memory, concurrency, and scaled it outward: to many cores sharing memory under one kernel (SMP, coherence, NUMA), to many boxes passing messages (multicomputers, MPI), and finally to independent machines coordinating despite partial failure (distributed systems, consensus, CAP). The recurring lesson is that the fundamentals do not change with scale; only their difficulty and their names do."}]
4a:["$","div","5",{"className":"flex gap-3 rounded-lg border-l-[3px] border-accent bg-accent/10 px-4 py-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"aria-hidden":true,"className":"mt-0.5 select-none text-lg","children":"✎"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.98rem] leading-[1.6] text-ink/90","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-hand text-lg font-bold text-accent","children":["What is next",": "]}],"Part G turns from principles to practice: case studies of real systems that put all of this together, how actual operating systems and large-scale platforms are built and where the ideas from every previous part show up in production."]}]]}]
4b:["$","figure",null,{"className":"lg:sticky lg:top-8","children":[["$","$L34",null,{"url":"https://96375ehh.ap-southeast.insforge.app/api/storage/buckets/os-notes/objects/ch33%2F08-why-engineers-care.jpg?v=b4601581931ca7d18d8e3c3ebef2a38c","alt":"Handwritten A4 diagram: a vertical \"same idea, bigger scale\" ladder with paired rungs. Bottom rung: \"mutex on one machine\" on the left, arrow up to \"distributed lock (etcd/Redis)\" on the right. Next rung: \"threads sharing memory\" → \"microservices sharing a network\". Next: \"cache coherence across cores\" → \"replica consistency across nodes\". Top: \"one OS scheduling threads\" → \"orchestrator scheduling containers across a cluster\". A side arrow points off the page to a box labelled \"Computer Networking course, the wire details\" and a forward arrow labelled \"Part G: Case Studies\"."}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"mt-2 text-center text-xs italic text-mute","children":"Tap to enlarge"}]]}]
